    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been looking at the inclusions under the scope. Some of these shards have these really strange, irregular pitting patterns that don't seem to follow a consistent pattern of organic decay or thermal shock. It's almost like the surface was disturbed or picked at while it was still in a semi-soft state before firing. Do you have any ideas on what might cause that kind of localized surface disruption?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Localized irregular pitting could be caused by several factors. One possibility is post-firing treatment, where workers or potters might have intentionally or unintentionally disturbed the surface during a tempering or drying process. This could include scratching or applying non-uniform forces while the clay was still plastic and porous. Another possibility is the partial firing of pieces, where the surface may have been transferred to a kiln that operated at different temperatures, causing premature sintering and potential disturbance. Also, variations in heating rates during firing can result in uneven expansion and contraction, possibly leaving pitting or grooving patterns. Compare your findings with other examples from the Late Helladic period—sometimes, contextual information can provide clues based on the techniques prevalent during that era.",
